Z||000043||Capt Fiero||09-25-2001||10:14 AM||||I really want to know where are the street footage is, I sent out 370meg of footage that I had and I guess a ton of people downloaded it, I want to see some smokey fiero burnouts and races.<P>------------------<BR>David<BR>85GT<BR>     [IMG]http://photos.onebigvillage.com/pubimage.asp?id_=32415 [/IMG]||||reg||1
Z||000044||Shaun41178||09-25-2001||06:57 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by OcalaFiero:<BR><B>  He was probably referring to the fact that the G Tech is pretty inaccurate in measuring 1/4 mile and 0-60 times. You may have gotten lucky and had it come out close to your "time sheet", but, G-Tech's are notorious for being inaccurate. This is coming from an engineer that has compared them to all of the other methods of measuring time/speed/distance. In college we did an experiment by taking 3 Gtechs (ramndomly selected) and placed all 3 in the same car, at the same time, ran a quarter mile, and got 3 widely different results. But G Techs do look cool in the mall/high school parking lot I guess. <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Um yea my "time sheets" are actual printouts from Norwalk raceway park in Central Ohio.  All were within less then .1 accurate.  But that is probably cause I didn't mount it wrong.  I would show them to you but you aren't worth my time.  <P>Wow youy placed 3 g-techs in a car and tested them?  That is super scientific there mr. "engineer"  Umm the directions say to place the g-tech in the middle of the windshield just below the rearview mirror.  I am sure you had all three in the same spot on the windshield?  You probably used that engineer head of yours and mounted in front of the passenger seat.  How scientific.  But not the scientific method cause it doesn't sound like you had a control group in there somewhere so your results are bogus.  If you would learn to follow directions you probably would have had different results.  Sounds like you messed up on that one pal.<P>Take your "I wannabe cool but only have 70 posts so I am really not" @ss and step back before you get embarressed too bad.  <P>Peace and Iron Duck forever<P><P>------------------<BR>Shaun 85 GT with nitrous<BR>0-60 in 5.6 seconds<BR>Shaun41178@hotmail.com<BR>PORN FOREVER!||||reg||1
Z||000045||ocalafiero1||09-25-2001||07:43 PM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by Shaun41178:<BR>[B] Um yea my "time sheets" are actual printouts from Norwalk raceway park in Central Ohio.  All were within less then .1 accurate.  But that is probably cause I didn't mount it wrong.  I would show them to you but you aren't worth my time.[B]<P>I know what time sheets are, I don't doubt that you've gone to Norwalk, Hell at least you try to back up your boasts, that I commend you for. I don't need you to show me how to mount a G-Tech or any of the "real" devices that are used to test cars, I've done it plenty of times before.<P>[B] youy placed 3 g-techs in a car and tested them?  That is super scientific there mr. "engineer"  Umm the directions say to place the g-tech in the middle of the windshield just below the rearview mirror.  I am sure you had all three in the same spot on the windshield?[B]<P>No, they were mounted one above the other directly below the mirror. 3 runs were done, with the positions switched after each run.<P>[B]probably used that engineer head of yours and mounted in front of the passenger seat.  How scientific.[B]<P>See above<P>[B] But not the scientific method cause it doesn't sound like you had a control group in there somewhere so your results are bogus.[B]<P>There was a control group, the 3 units were tested individually, and the car was used with a rolling wheel test unit, a photocell and computer setup also good old fashioned stopwatch tests.<P>[B]If you would learn to follow directions you probably would have had different results.  Sounds like you messed up on that one pal.[B]<P>Following directions is not really a problem, when the professor we worked with was involved in the original design of the accelerometers used in the G-Tech, so I think that we were able to follow them.<P>[B]Take your "I wannabe cool but only have 70 posts so I am really not" @ss and step back before you get embarressed too bad.[B]<P>I guess someday I might be able to have as many posts as you, huh? Then again most of yours are wortless drivel and trolling. Ok, shaun, you win, please don't be mean to me.  <P>||||reg||1
Z||000046||Slammed Fiero||09-25-2001||07:58 PM||||hate to burst the GTEC Nay Sayers bubbles , But there have been tests done with a GTEC vs. a Vericom (By Sports car mag , Grassroots motorsports , OnTrack magazine...)and the GTEC was very close to the Vericom's readouts. If you don't know what a Vericom is , look at it as a $1500 to $2200 GTEC depending on options , it replaced the 3rd wheel previously used by magazines.<P>Now the big boy on the block uses a GPS setup to track the cars speed and distance covered to calculate performance data.<P>I have used 3 different GTECS in the course of a year and always got similar numbers (within .3 of a sec)(Autocross club unit , Chris Moores Gtec and a friends unit) So I believe it to be an accurate unit. I have also used a vericom before (autocross club has one)I have never compared the 2 , but the Vericom always gave me similar #'s to the GTEC.<P><BR>JM ||||reg||1
Z||000047||OcalaFiero||09-26-2001||07:58 AM||||The G-Tech's not a complete P.O.S., (although the commercial for it is pretty ridiculous). I just thought old (oops, young) Shaun needed to be brought down a notch. Like I said, the testing we did (this was research that GTech's developers were sponsoring through our professor) showed them to be pretty accurate, but there were more fluctuations in times than with some of the more tried and true methods. But for $125.00 it's not too bad of a deal. ||||reg||1
